From 5c98f03de45efa5feafe60941ccf332882914890 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bjorn Pagen <11238136+bjornpagen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 21:01:25 -0600
Subject: [PATCH v1 1/4] Let extensions restrict the parameterization of a
 relation's paths.

A path's parameterization, the set of outer relations it requires, is a
planner decision of its own: whether the join clauses to some outer
relations are evaluated inside a scan, typically as index conditions, or
above it.  The PGS_* bits in pgs_mask can steer scan types, join methods
and the like, but they cannot steer this, because it is not a strategy
but a choice of relid set.

Add two fields beside pgs_mask, RelOptInfo.pgs_required_outer and
RelOptInfo.pgs_forbidden_outer.  A path that is not parameterized by
every relation in the first, or that is parameterized by a relation in
the second other than one of the rel's own lateral_relids, counts as one
more disabled node.  Both default to empty, which permits every
parameterization, so nothing changes unless an extension sets them, from
build_simple_rel_hook or joinrel_setup_hook as it would pgs_mask.

The check is made in add_path(), add_path_precheck(), add_partial_path()
and add_partial_path_precheck(), where every path of a rel arrives, and
in reparameterize_path(), whose paths deliberately bypass add_path(),
rather than in the cost functions.  That way it applies equally to paths
whose costs core never computes, such as foreign, custom and Append
paths.  It also has to happen no later than add_path(): when a
parameterized path costs no less than an unparameterized one, add_path()
lets the unparameterized path dominate and discards the parameterized
path before any join is considered, so a restriction applied only while
planning joins could not bring it back.  A disabled path survives as a
last resort, as any disabled path does, so the restriction can never
cause planner failure.

pg_plan_advice will use this to enforce advice about parameterization.

While here, make reparameterize_path() honor IndexOptInfo.disabled for
the index paths it copies, as create_index_path() does; previously a
disabled index could be used through a reparameterized child path of an
Append.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
